//! run
//! flags: -I ../../src -I support
//! stdout:
//| ok
/// A build of a closure of two modules, as far as the module that `-S` stops
/// at. The driver gives back everything it takes: the module set of the whole
/// closure, the checker over it and the emitter. `-S` also spawns no clang for
/// each round.
import lifetime;

//
// support/lifetime.ft states the rule of the probe, its view and the
// size of a round. Each file tests one driver mode. This keeps every program
// below the harness limit of 60 s under load.
//
// Removing any listed release moves the live view past lifetime.BOUND.
// driver.compile_entry releases its pool, session, and analysis.
// driver.load_closure releases its pool in a deferred loop.
// driver.std_dir_beside runs `defer del(b.data)`.
// check.check_free runs `del(ck->syms)`, `del(ck->values)`, and
// `types.type_table_free`. gen.gen_free releases `named`, `funcs`, `out`,
// `allocas`, `body`, `scratch`, and its pool. At b6676ced another probe also
// went red for each of these deletions.
// Every release of the six modes was measured this way, one deletion at a
// time, against all six tests. support/lifetime.ft gives the release count and
// the measurement. It also names the releases whose deletion moved no probe,
// and says why for each.
fn main() i32 {
    lifetime.write_runtime();
    lifetime.write_file("closure.ft",
        "import helper;\nfn main() i32 {\n    return helper.one();\n}\n");
    lifetime.write_file("helper.ft", "fn one() i32 {\n    return 1;\n}\n");
    string[3] mut build = {"fort", "-S", "closure.ft"};
    assert(lifetime.measure(build[0..3]) < lifetime.BOUND);
    println("ok");
    return 0;
}
